## 2. Editor notes (no number, dose, threshold or clinical claim)

- E1 "Editor note: call the transplant team before changing, holding or stopping any immunosuppressant." Lives in tpe-pres body (once) and critical action 4 (once). Nowhere else on the page.
- E2 "Editor note: open Status epilepticus for general seizure steps." tpe-pres-seizure.
- E3 "Editor note: open Hypertensive emergency (adult) for blood pressure, the Seizure hub for a first seizure and the Altered mental status hub for the confusion work-up." tpe-pres-links. Links with a reason each.
- E4 "Editor note: related pages are toxicity and levels, brain and high blood pressure." tpe-pres-links (tpe-cni-toxicity, tpe-cni-brain, tpe-cni-hypertension).
- E5 "Editor note: disposition is decided with the treating transplant team." tpe-pres-links body (last line) and recommendation (last words, outside the bold).

## 3. Gaps awaiting review papers (the page says plainly it does not provide these; they are listed here, not answered)

- Diagnostic criteria for PRES in transplant recipients (clinical or radiological). Sources read give description only (heart narrative e64; cyclosporine labels, white-matter changes).
- An imaging protocol (modality, timing). The heart guideline asks for imaging only for encephalopathy late after heart transplantation (e65, 2010 column, Class I, C, "Continuing approval without change" in 2023).
- Blood pressure value, rate of lowering and agent for PRES. Heart guideline says only "gradual".
- How far to reduce or withdraw the calcineurin inhibitor, for how long, and what to substitute. The heart guideline names substitution and warns of inadequate immunosuppression; labels say consider reduction or discontinuation.
- Which antiepileptic drugs are acceptable. The heart narrative says what to avoid and names no acceptable agent.
- Guidance for kidney, liver and lung recipients beyond the labels. The heart guideline is heart-only; the liver guideline has Table 4 only.
- Trough levels in PRES (heart narrative says patients "generally have supratherapeutic levels"; labels say symptoms "may be associated" with troughs at or above range; no source gives a number).
- Review papers (PMC4998758 is saved in this folder; not read for this draft by instruction) are the expected source for all of the above.

## 4. Source differences (stated on the page or overlay; none resolved)

1. Tacrolimus labels print an action ("consider dosage reduction or discontinuation ... if neurotoxicity occurs") and tie symptoms to troughs at or above range. Cyclosporine labels print no instruction; they describe the changes as reversible upon discontinuation in most cases and improved after dose reduction in some, list predisposing factors, and say liver recipients appear more susceptible than kidney recipients.
2. Tacrolimus Highlights are firmer than the sections: Prograf "reduce or discontinue PROGRAF"; Astagraf XL and Envarsus XR "reduce dosage or discontinue"; sections say "consider". The node uses the section wording.
3. Section numbers differ: Prograf 5.6, Astagraf XL 5.7, Envarsus XR 5.6. Envarsus XR prints "seizure, and coma" (comma); Prograf and Astagraf XL "seizure and coma".
4. Neoral and Gengraf versus Sandimmune: "high dose" versus "high-dosage" methylprednisolone and corticosteroids; "post-marketing" versus "postmarketing"; "reduction of dose" versus "reduction of dosage"; "movement disorders and psychiatric" versus "movement disorders, and psychiatric"; Sandimmune omits "occurring in transplant patients more frequently than in other indications". Neoral and Gengraf print the Neurotoxicity paragraph under Warnings, Kidney, Liver, and Heart Transplant; Sandimmune under Warnings.
5. Heart guideline versus labels: the guideline (Class I, C) says withdrawal or reduction of the calcineurin inhibitor "along with substitution for an alternate immunosuppressive agent or an alternate CNI" and, in the narrative, warns against sub-optimal anti-rejection dosing; the tacrolimus labels say consider reduction or discontinuation. 2010 and 2023 columns differ: 2010 "reduction of CNI doses or substitution with an alternative CNI"; 2023 adds "gradual blood pressure reduction" and "withdrawal".
6. Heart narrative says to avoid phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine and valproic acid; the tacrolimus labels list phenytoin, carbamazepine and phenobarbital as strong inducers and say to "Increase [the] dose and monitor tacrolimus whole blood trough concentrations" (they do not say to avoid them); cyclosporine labels list them as decreasing cyclosporine concentrations. Neither source says which to choose.
7. Nicardipine: tacrolimus labels (mild or moderate CYP3A inhibitor; label action monitor trough and reduce the dose if needed) and cyclosporine labels (increase concentrations) versus the app consult's nicardipine node (App content, row A13). The page states both and does not say which is right.

## 5. Statements about existing consults (read from src/data/trees/; not citations)

- status-epilepticus.ts line 331 (node se-2nd-line-choice body): "Levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, and valproate had equivalent efficacy (~47% seizure cessation at 60 min)." Shown on the page.
- status-epilepticus.ts line 466 (node se-phenobarbital body): "Use when ESETT agents (levetiracetam, valproate, fosphenytoin) are unavailable or contraindicated." Shown on the page.
- status-epilepticus.ts line 413 (node se-fosphenytoin body) gives a fosphenytoin dose (not copied); line 769 (STATUS_EPILEPTICUS_CRITICAL_ACTIONS) names levetiracetam for second-line ASM (dose not copied). Not shown on the page: a reader could take it as the consult choosing; the page shows the two nodes that name the heart-guideline drugs.
- status-epilepticus.ts: text search for transplant, immunosuppress, calcineurin, tacrolimus, cyclosporin: no hits. Not shown on the page (words).
- hypertensive-emergency-adult.ts line 169 (node hypertensive-emergency-adult-agent-nicardipine body): "Nicardipine IV infusion is a common titratable choice when no exception applies." Shown on the page. Same file line 153 (node agent-by-organ body): "General or brain, no exception: a calcium-channel blocker infusion"; line 170 recommendation "Titratable IV calcium-channel blocker infusion with continuous monitoring, for hypertensive emergency without a specific exception." Link target is the agent-by-organ node.
- hypertensive-emergency-adult.ts line 16 (critical action): "In the general case, do not drop SBP by more than 25% in the first hour, then reduce gradually over 24 to 48 hours". Not shown: the heart guideline's PRES statement says "gradual blood pressure reduction" with no rate; juxtaposing them would invite a comparison no source makes. Text search for transplant, immunosuppress, calcineurin, tacrolimus, cyclosporin: no hits.
- seizure-hub.ts and altered-mental-status-hub.ts: no transplant, immunosuppress, calcineurin, tacrolimus or cyclosporin text (search). seizure-hub.ts lines 167-168 and 339 say "Avoid phenytoin" for toxic seizures; no named default drug at the hub level that the sources caution about. Linked without an App content line.

## 6. Left out, and why

- Any immunosuppressant dose, any anticonvulsant or antihypertensive dose (no source needed; the page gives none).
- Tacrolimus and cyclosporine Medication Guide "nervous system problems" wording and the adverse-reaction frequency tables: no extra clinical statement; counselling 17.6 is in the overlay.
- ISHLT Table 5 (antiepileptic drugs under drugs that decrease levels) and the IATDMCT Table 2 inducer list: duplicate the label rows; the toxicity page holds the interaction lists.
- ISHLT narrative on ICU delirium and the cause list for seizures beyond the overlay bullet; psychiatric and peripheral nerve sections: not PRES.
- AASLD/AST candidate-evaluation hepatic encephalopathy text: pre-transplant liver disease, not calcineurin inhibitor neurotoxicity.
- Review papers saved in the folder (PRES_CNI_in_SOT_review_PMC4998758.html and the two TMA files): not read, by instruction.
- Hypomagnesemia and cholesterol correction: the heart guideline says "correction of hypomagnesemia, if present" (kept) and no source read gives a level or replacement (stated on the toxicity page).
